/** \page security Security Policy

\section security_default Secure by default

Since Zorba 5.0.0 a Zorba instance runs queries under
<tt>zorba::SecurityPolicy::secure()</tt> unless the application says
otherwise. Under the secure policy a query cannot:

- read or write files (<tt>fn:doc</tt>, <tt>fn:unparsed-text</tt>, stop
  words, thesauri and the EXPath file module on files);
- use the network (http, https, ftp and ftps documents and text, the
  http-client and ftp-client modules);
- import modules or schemas over the network;
- expand external entities, an external DTD subset or XInclude in XML input;
- load native libraries that implement external functions;
- read environment variables (<tt>fn:environment-variable</tt> and
  <tt>fn:available-environment-variables</tt> return the empty sequence, and
  <tt>ZORBA_URI_PATH</tt> / <tt>ZORBA_LIB_PATH</tt> are ignored).

Queries can still use everything that does not leave the process: the
in-memory store and collections, documents and items the application passes
in, the built-in modules, and modules and schemas found in the trusted URI
and library paths (the built-in paths and the ones set with
<tt>StaticContext::setURIPath()</tt>, <tt>setLibPath()</tt> and
<tt>setModulePaths()</tt>). Recursion depth (1024 calls) and expression
nesting depth (1500) are limited. There is no default timeout.

Zorba 4.x and earlier ran every query with all of these capabilities.

\section security_allow Allowing capabilities

The application grants capabilities through the C++ API; a query can never
widen the policy it runs under. Set the instance default with
<tt>Zorba::setSecurityPolicy()</tt>, or a policy for one static context (and
the queries compiled with it) with <tt>StaticContext::setSecurityPolicy()</tt>:

\code
Zorba* z = Zorba::getInstance(StoreManager::getStore());

// Read documents from one directory, give up after 10 seconds.
z->setSecurityPolicy(SecurityPolicy::secure()
    .allow(Capability::FileRead).addReadPath("/srv/data")
    .setTimeout(std::chrono::seconds(10)));

// One context may also call one web service.
StaticContext_t sctx = z->createStaticContext();
sctx->setSecurityPolicy(SecurityPolicy::secure()
    .allow(Capability::FileRead).addReadPath("/srv/data")
    .allow(Capability::Network).addNetworkPrefix("https://api.example.com/v1/")
    .allow(Capability::NativeModules));   // the http-client module
XQuery_t q = z->compileQuery(query, sctx);
\endcode

The capabilities are listed in <tt>zorba::Capability</tt>. Network access to
loopback, private and link-local addresses is denied unless a network prefix
names such a host or <tt>SecurityPolicy::setAllowPrivateAddresses(true)</tt>
is called.

\section security_optout Restoring the old behavior

An application that trusts its queries and needs the behavior of Zorba 4.x
sets the unrestricted policy once, before compiling queries:

\code
z->setSecurityPolicy(SecurityPolicy::unrestricted());
\endcode

\section security_errors Errors

A resource the policy denies is reported with the error the specification
prescribes for a resource that cannot be retrieved (for example
<tt>err:FODC0002</tt> for <tt>fn:doc</tt>, <tt>err:XQST0059</tt> for a module
import), and the message says that the security policy denied it and which
capability is missing. Importing a module that needs a capability the policy
does not allow (the file, http-client and ftp-client modules, native
modules) raises <tt>zerr:ZXQP0067</tt>. See the documentation of
<tt>zorba::SecurityPolicy</tt> for the complete list.

\section security_cli Command line

The <tt>zorba</tt> command-line tool keeps running queries with the
unrestricted policy; <tt>--secure</tt>, <tt>--allow</tt> and <tt>--deny</tt>
restrict it (see \ref commandLineSecurity).

*/
